Falsifies `system_tests::user::map_revocation_window_tests::a_sweep_inside_a_map_into_leaves_no_mapping`.

Architecture: aarch64, riscv64, x86_64

The defect is the tree as it stood until 2026-10-04 (UTC): the patch puts `AddressSpace::MAP_INTO`
back to reading its frame with `sched::current_cap` before the seam, outside the mapping registry,
and mapping and recording only afterwards. The sweep held at the seam deletes the capability and
scans a log the mapping is not yet in, and the mapping is recorded after it, alive.

This was the live tree, not an invented break. The revocation-race lane recorded it reasoned on
2026-10-04; this test drove it red on aarch64 under both `PageFrame::REVOKE` and
`MemoryRegion::DESTROY` before the fix existed.

Expected to fail: `assert!(!REPORT.mapped.load(..), "an AddressSpace::MAP_INTO held while a {sweep:?} ran kept its mapping ...")`, on the `Revoke` round.
Replayed red on riscv64 on 2026-10-04 (UTC) against the fixed tree, at
`map_revocation_window_tests.rs:307`, the assertion predicted.

**The assertions above the headline stay green under this patch**: the seam was reached, and the
sweep reached the builder's own table.

Nothing here is architecture-specific, so one patch serves every leg.

diff --git a/kernel/src/syscall.rs b/kernel/src/syscall.rs
index 98f833e7c..4092fbcd0 100644
--- a/kernel/src/syscall.rs
+++ b/kernel/src/syscall.rs
@@ -900,11 +900,11 @@ fn address_space_map_into(
